+## NarrationTake
+`scene_id, text_sha256, cache_key, characters, start_seconds, duration_seconds,
+scene_seconds, speed, cache_hit`
+
+One scene's narration inside a track that was spoken scene by scene. A take is
+not an asset of its own: the run holds one narration file, and these say which
+words were spoken for which scene, where they sit on the timeline, how long
+they turned out to be and how fast they were delivered. `speed` is null when
+the provider's default was used. `end_seconds`, `headroom_seconds` and
+`fits_scene` are derived. `VoiceAsset.takes` is empty for a Reel spoken as one
+take, which is what `VOICE_TAKES=whole` does and what happens by default.
+
+The run's request log gains one `audio.speech` record per take that was
+actually generated. Words a later scene repeats at the same speed make no
+request at all, in this run or in a later one that finds the take cached, so a
+run that re-lays an unchanged script over a rescaled storyboard adds nothing
+to the log. Counting `success` records is counting paid speech calls.
+
+One entry, as it appears under `takes` in `voice.json`:
+
+```json
+{
+  "scene_id": "S-03",
+  "text_sha256": "…",
+  "cache_key": "…",
+  "characters": 39,
+  "start_seconds": 8.0,
+  "duration_seconds": 2.6,
+  "scene_seconds": 5.0,
+  "speed": null,
+  "cache_hit": false
+}
+```
+
+A `voice.json` written by a stopped round is a normal record with `status`
+`failed`, `error` carrying the provider's own words, and `takes` holding the
+scenes that were spoken before the refusal — four entries for a Reel of eight
+that was refused at the fifth. It is not a partial track: `path` is unset,
+because no audio was laid onto the timeline. Read it as a receipt for what the
+run has already paid for rather than as a result, and note that the take of the
+refused scene is not among the entries, since nothing came back for it.
+
+The request log tells the same story a call at a time: one `audio.speech`
+record with outcome `success` per take that was spoken, then one with outcome
+`rejected` for the take that was not, and nothing at all for the scenes after
+it. A run that is attempted twice therefore has two runs of records in one
+log, and the second is shorter than the first by every take the first one
+managed to speak.
+
